2. How does your backend forward chunks to the browser?
Interview answer
"After receiving each chunk from Gemini, I pass it to the onChunk callback in my Express route.

Inside that callback, I convert the chunk into JSON format and use res.write() to send it to the browser as an SSE event.

I use the data: field and a double newline to follow the SSE event format.

I continue sending chunks using res.write() while keeping the HTTP response open. Once Gemini finishes generating the response, I send a completion event and call res.end()."

Understand it with your code:
(chunk) => {
  fullResponse += chunk;

  const sseData = JSON.stringify({
    chunk,
    type: 'chunk'
  });

  res.write(`data: ${sseData}\n\n`);
}


Suppose the callback receives:
chunk = "Node.js";


Your backend sends:
data: {"chunk":"Node.js","type":"chunk"}


The next chunk might be:
data: {"chunk":" is a runtime","type":"chunk"}


Both events are sent through the same HTTP response.

When streaming finishes:
res.write(`data: ${doneData}\n\n`);
res.end();


The done event tells the frontend that generation is complete. res.end() closes the response.

3. How does your frontend identify and display each chunk?
Interview answer
"On the frontend, I use the Fetch API to call my streaming POST endpoint.

I access the response stream using response.body.getReader() and use TextDecoder to convert the incoming bytes into readable text.

Since network chunks may not contain complete SSE events, I maintain a buffer and split the data using the SSE event separator, which is a double newline.

After identifying a complete event, I remove the data: prefix and parse the JSON.

I check the event type. If it is a chunk event, I append the received text to the existing response and update the UI. If it is a done event, I stop the loading state."

Understand it with your code:

First, get the stream reader:
const reader = response.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();


Read incoming data:
const { done, value } = await reader.read();


Convert bytes into text:
const text = decoder.decode(value, {
  stream: true
});


Maintain the buffer:
buffer += text;


Separate complete events:
const messages = buffer.split('\n\n');
buffer = messages[messages.length - 1];


Parse the event:
const parsed = JSON.parse(jsonStr);


Identify the event:
if (parsed.type === 'chunk') {
  fullResponse += parsed.chunk;
  responseDiv.textContent = fullResponse;
}


The frontend keeps appending the new text, so users see the answer appearing progressively.

